ent. No, you you can\'t put a quantitative piece, you know, or or or number on what the psychology of investors is going to be. So, in those hot markets, you need to take advantage and you have to use the right tools for that scenario. And one of those tools that I\'ve learned to put a lot of emphasis on, and it\'s critical, is time. Everyone will look at price. There\'s two things when you get into trade. There\'s time and there\'s price. And if you just look at price, and this is something, you know, William O\'Neil would would warn about, these stocks can move so quickly that just the the intensity of the price move can throw you off, which I think is what happened to the vast majority of people in this cycle. And so, I look at price and I look at time coming out of these bases. If the move is real, if there\'s something of substance, and every cycle, like we mentioned, you you can go back at the auto makers, you can look at steel makers, you can look at railroads, you can look at jet engine manufacturers. Every cycle, the \'90s dot com it takes time for one, the average person to understand what\'s going on, and two, it takes time for institutions to have to build the adequate positions. Like Jason mentioned, you you know, they\'re they\'re in the indexes, you have to put it on. That takes weeks or months. I had a family member come over last night and he he\'s like a a proud value guy. And I asked him, you know, he was going on about he\'s buying the Canadian telcos cuz you know, maybe they\'ll turn them around, which you know, that\'s that\'s that\'s fine. It\'s his view. But I asked him, you know, like what how about AI, you know, I\'m just curious, you anything you have He\'s like, "Well, well, you know, he\'s like, I dabbled in Nvidia a little bit last year and made a little bit of money. So that was pretty good." So like before the vast majority of people and their psychology and everyone shifts and institutions can get positioned to size of substance in t